    "line": 1519,
    "label": "Exercise C.4 \u2014 How many runs, and which $R$?",
    "level": null,
    "source_sha256": "bf2b63f0065f7701027e820af7fe0eb6da905ae61e414e75741ba49867226a87",
    "source_text": "\n      \n        Exercise C.4 \u2014 How many runs, and which $R$?\n      \n      \n        Outcomes lie in $[0,1]$. (a) How many independent samples guarantee $|\\bar X_N-m|\\lt0.05$ with probability at least $0.99$, using Chebyshev with the worst-case variance and using Hoeffding? (b) Give a valid sub-Gaussian parameter for noise uniform on $[-0.2,0.2]$ and compare it with the standard deviation. (c) Why is noise uniform on $[0,0.2]$ not covered by (b)?\n        \n          Show answer\n          \n            (a) Chebyshev with $\\operatorname{Var}\\le\\tfrac14$: $\\frac{1/4}{N\\cdot0.0025}\\le0.01$ gives $N\\ge10000$. Hoeffding: $2e^{-2N\\cdot0.0025}\\le0.01$ gives $N\\ge\\ln(200)/0.005=1059.7$, so $1060$.\n            (b) Hoeffding's lemma: $R=(b-a)/2=0.2$. The standard deviation is $0.4/\\sqrt{12}=0.115$; for the uniform law the smallest valid $R$ actually equals $0.115$ (table in Section 5), so the lemma is conservative by a factor $\\sqrt3$.\n            (c) Its mean is $0.1\\ne0$. Sub-Gaussianity (and Hoeffding's lemma) require zero mean; the bias $0.1$ must be modelled separately, e.g. as a known offset or an unknown constant in the model.\n          \n        \n      \n    ",
    "text_sha256": "8a872115a9c560c6a054081ccade08d8c4ac799874508cd39a0fa10c0917ef8b"
  },
